  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 314 PM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nOn Tuesday, the frontal boundary will lift north into northern \nNebraska. Persistent clouds and the threat for showers and \nthunderstorms will carry over Tuesday into Tuesday evening with \nthe main threat across northern Nebraska. Highs Tuesday will \nrange from the lower 80s in the north to near 90 in the south. \nOn Wednesday, a strong shortwave trough will cross the northern \nRockies bringing a decent threat for thunderstorms to the \nDakotas. The current NBM forecast has some isolated pops across \nnorthwestern portions of the forecast area Wednesday night. The \nlatest deterministic GFS and to a lesser degree the EC soln does\ngenerate some QPF over NW and north central Nebraska Wednesday \nnight. The NBM barely has a 20% chance of exceeding 0.01\" of QPF\nacross the northwestern forecast area Wednesday night. That \nbeing said, will let the NBM initialization ride for now. High \npressure aloft will continue to retrograde to the west \nnorthwest, becoming anchored over the central and southern \nplains late this week into the weekend. This will lead to mainly\ndry conditions for Labor Day weekend with very warm \ntemperatures. High temperatures look to be well into the 90s \nacross the forecast area for Friday, Saturday and Sunday. \nOverall, the current high temperature forecast is running around\n10 degrees above normal for the first week of September.",
